A||||11||ChadMan||OK, handling wizards...||||5||
Z||000000||ChadMan||08-15-2000||05:51 PM||||I usually have a good seat-of-the-pants understanding of physics, but I'm having trouble with the following two questions:<P>88 Coupes (supposedly) have softer springs than the GT/Formula.  I replaced my saggy rear springs with Formula springs, but left the front springs alone.  Should this make my car understeer more (push, for you NASCAR guys) or oversteer more (loose) than a stock 88 Coupe?<P>88 Coupes have no rear sway bar like the GT/Formulas.  If, by chance, I were to find an 88 rear sway bar and attach it, would this make my car understeer more, or oversteer more?<P>I have guesses, but I'd rather hear from somebody who knows!  Thanks in advance.||||reg||5
Z||000001||tgowens||08-15-2000||06:54 PM||||yes||||reg||1
Z||000002||tgowens||08-15-2000||06:56 PM||||Hey ChadMan!! -- in other words, how the H*** should I know, I have an '87GT! -- but, i'm keeping this at the top for ya!! (I'm really interested in the answer too!)||||reg||1
Z||000003||filthyscarecrow||08-15-2000||07:16 PM||||stiffening the suspension reduces grip. stiffening the back would loosen it up. the car would be a little more loose than stock. adding a swaybar to the back would drastically loosen it. if you're going to add a rear swaybar, put a bigger bar on the front or stiffer springs in to balance it. you want to loosen the car to the point that it is neutral, but ideally not more. tkae it real easy the fist few days driving it and see how it feels. it will most likely not feel too different with the springs, but you will notice a sway bar right away...||||reg||1
Z||000004||Taijiguy||08-15-2000||08:18 PM||||I would put better springs up front, and put poly bushings on the front sway bar (both center and end links) and then add the rear sway bar. It should balance out OK, since the coupes are prone to oversteer anyway. You can make slight changes in handling by altering tire pressure as well. Lower pressuer will cause the tires to slip more, increased pressure will make them grip better. (somewhat anyway) So, with the improvements you've already made, if you find it breaks loose in the rear, you can always increase tire pressure in the rear, like maybe to 40 psi, and leave the front at 32, and see what it does then, change it as you need to for balance until you can make the other mods.<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierocrazy||08-15-2000||10:16 PM||||i say the tighter the better, that in dependant rear oves to be tight||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||08-16-2000||09:26 AM||||So if I'm hearing you guys correctly:<P>Firmer rear springs/sway bar = loose<BR>Firmer front springs/sway bar = tight<P>This would seem to make sense - when I bought the car, it pushed really bad.  After I replaced the rear springs, it was much more neutral, but had a tendency to break the rear end loose if I drove it hard through corners, which can be hard to recover in a mid-engine car.  So I replaced the rear 195/70R14's with 215/60R14's and now I'm happy.  I run 35 psi all around.<P>Thanks guys!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Taijiguy||08-16-2000||11:20 AM||||Ummm...actually, I don't know if it's nit-picking of me to follow up your comment or not, but let's be somewhat specific.<P>Tighter is better. That's a given. However, when you're trying to balance a system, you have to understand that certain things have more imapct than others, and each change affects everything else.<P>The stiffer springs in the rear will make it tighter, obviously, and should help to make the rear end hold better. However, the improvement to the rear will effect the front. The suggestions I made for example, were made in an effort to help balance a system with a drastic imbalance. <P>What you have to keep in mind is, the rear end has one way to go when rounding a corner, and that's wherever the front leads it. When it can no longer follow the front end, it breaks loose, and moves outwards. This has the effect of causing the front end to turn sharper inwards, since you're effectively changing the full direction of the car. In some cases, this is great if you can control it, which is ULTIMATELY the goal. Of course you want the car to handle well, and to have as high a threshold as possible before breaking loose, but the ability to control the car when you're at the peak of it's ability to stick is the true key. When you can maintain control by feathering the throttle, and by countersteering, you have a good balance in your suspension. Right now, you have probably little to no control, since you have no idea how the car will respond- if it'll oversteer or understeer. The suggestions are primarily to help balance it out so it's somewhat more predictable.<BR>A blanket statement that tighter suspension=looser handling is technically inaccurate, since it's all relative. The tighter rear end will be loose in relation to the front end trying to push. The front will push, but you will be better able to apply power to get the rear end to come loose a little, which while counter the looseness in the front end. Once the front gets tightened, you will hold tighter in the corner, not oversteering so much, which will have the affect of making the rear tighter, since you won't have to compensate for oversteer. I know this is probably confusing as hell since it's hard to explain. The bottom line is; Tighter IS better, but only to an extent, since you still want the suspension to follow the contour of the road, and not skip around. <BR>I hope this helped SOME and didn't make things worse..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Later<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000008||ChadMan||08-16-2000||01:55 PM||||Every bit of info helps, Taiji!  We may be miscommunicating on tight v. loose, too - I'm using tight=understeer, loose=oversteer, not tight=firm suspension, loose=soft suspension.<P>Ideally, I'd prefer handling that is nearly neutral with a slight tendency toward understeer (a good example would be the Porsche 924/944).  From the factory, Fieros (like most cars) are set up for fairly heavy understeer.  However, this wastes a lot of the tires' available grip - I don't like my front tires squealing and slipping before the back ones even start working hard.  My 84 was set up like this and I never bothered trying to optimize it by playing with springs/tires/sway bars etc.<P>When I bought the 88 Coupe, it had worn-out original shocks/struts and rear springs and was borderline unsafe to drive.  I bought KYB's all around and figured I'd replace the rear springs with Formula/GT springs for durability and better handling.  But the car became somewhat tail-happy (loose), which I cured by going with fatter rear tires.  The car is still slightly tail-happy, but it gives plenty of warning and is pretty easy to recover should the tail start to step out.  Maybe I should get a set of Formula/GT springs for the front, too?<P>Why do I care about all this?  Well, I have only 98 hp (for now), so I have to get my jollies by going around corners fast.  (All you potential Nader voters, please spare me any safety-Nazi flaming.)||||reg||1
Z||000009||Taijiguy||08-16-2000||02:13 PM||||You don't have to convince me ChadMan about handling. I was (functionally) lowering cars back in the 70's while all my high school buddies were jacking the rear ends up. They all thought I was crazy until they realized I could go just as fast as them in the straights, and even FASTER around the corners. (how many truly stright roads are there anyway, huh?)<BR>My setup currently has 2" lower springs all around, with BIG sway bars front and rear, and Koni's all around. It's set up about as any car I've ever owned, and rides like a coal truck. But the handling is nothing short of amazing. A twitch to the wheel yields NO body roll, and a responsiveness I've never felt in a street car before. And I've had some nice handling cars in my life.<BR>I'd say another place you'll see some real gains in handling would be to get those 60 series tires offa there, and get some 50 series rubber. Will sure cut down on that sidewall roll.<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000010||TheFiero64||08-16-2000||05:08 PM||||I didn't think that 88 coupes had different spring. My 88 seems to have a good stance and handling with the v6 in it. I knew about the sway bar not on coupes but thought the rest was the same. I learn something every day.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000011||ChadMan||08-17-2000||08:59 AM||||Well, that's why I said "supposedly" in my first post.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>The 88 press materials said the GT & Formula had the WS6 suspension, which includes "special shocks and struts, special springs, special bushings, and special control arms."<P>Sadly, the press materials don't say what's "special" about any of these pieces.  What could be special about control arms?  My Coupe's control arms look exactly like any 88 Formula or GT's control arms I've ever seen.<p>[This message has been edited by ChadMan (edited 08-17-2000).]||||reg||1
